# Delivery Source Tree Contract

schema_version: 3
status: canonical-path-baseline
root: apps/governance-operations-console/src/domain-workspaces/delivery

This is the canonical source-tree contract for the Delivery domain workspace
path model. It records the implemented Delivery architecture paths, not a
mechanical relocation of the previous legacy tree.

No exceptions are allowed in the final Delivery source tree path model. Any
Delivery path outside this contract is a source-tree violation. New Delivery
code must use this tree and naming model.

This contract proves path ownership. Extraction grain, public boundary
discipline, product-app host boundaries, and surface/workflow consistency are
governed by the normalization rules in `operation-workbench-contract.md` and
by the baseline decisions below.

Naming rules:

- Prefer human readability over path-only DRY naming. File names must remain
  understandable in editor tabs, search results, import lists, and review diffs
  where the full folder tree may not be visible.
- Boundary folders provide ownership context, but important files may repeat
  the surface, workflow, or business concept when that improves readability.
- Avoid empty duplication such as repeating `delivery` in every internal file.
  Public workspace boundary files may carry `delivery-workspace` because they
  are imported outside the Delivery folder and act as external-facing entry
  points.
- Avoid generic major-component names such as `surface.tsx`, `view.tsx`, or
  `modal.tsx` when the file is likely to be opened, searched, or imported
  outside a tiny local folder. Use names such as `home-surface.tsx`,
  `refinement-session-modal.tsx`, or `work-design-hub-view.tsx`.
- Domain files, when extracted, name business concepts and rules.
- Application files, when extracted, name use cases, routing, commands, and
  projections of intent. Application files do not render React.
- Presentation files name UI components, workflow views, local view models, and
  CSS modules.
- Product adapter files name the mapping direction between Delivery and the
  product app.
- Test-only, fixture-only, and local-runtime files keep explicit suffixes such
  as `.fixture.ts`, `-receipt.ts`, `-command.ts`, and `-transition.ts`.

Normalization rules:

- The allowed path list is a source-tree boundary, not a quality certificate.
  A file inside an allowed path violates the normalization contract when it
  owns too many unrelated responsibilities.
- Comparable Delivery areas must use the same extraction grammar unless the
  difference is justified by a real domain boundary. Work Design and Refinement
  may differ in feature count, but their workflow scaffolds must make ownership
  of hub, session, controller, shell, step, support, artifact,
  product host, and view-model code obvious.
- Surface files should compose. A surface that owns selection state, modal
  routing, mutation drafts, derived rows, action copy, and panel rendering must
  be split by ownership before new behavior is added.
- A multi-panel surface must split panel renderers and panel-specific
  projections. A single view-model may compose them, but should not own several
  unrelated panel families itself.
- A config/control surface must make selection, current values, mutation draft,
  inspector/selected panel, and mutation dialog ownership visible.
- Workflow step folders hold routeable step entry points and step-local
  subcomponents only. Durable outputs move to `artifacts/`; reusable app hosts
  move to `embedded-products/`; reusable non-routeable workflow support moves
  to `support/`.
- `work-model/` owns domain command contracts, validation, transitions, and
  persisted workflow/session/artifact DTOs. It must not
  collect transient product-app interaction types such as drag state, resize
  state, selection boxes, tool drawers, hover state, or other UI-only app
  state unless that state is intentionally persisted and recorded.
- Product-app public APIs are imported from `product-adapters/` or explicit
  `embedded-products/<product>/` host edges. Other direct product-app imports
  are source-tree violations unless they are classified and recorded.
- `index.ts` is allowed only at true public boundaries. Explicit exports are
  preferred. Wildcard exports are allowed only for deliberate type/read-model
  facades or stable adapter facades whose whole module surface is intentionally
  public.

Architecture baseline decisions:

- `presentation/surfaces/home/` uses panel-family projections. Status,
  attention queue, recent activity, and agent response projection live in
  focused model files. `home-view-model.ts` is the thin composition and public
  import point for the Home surface.
- `presentation/surfaces/catalog/` exposes config/control presentation
  boundaries. `catalog-surface.tsx` composes the control layout,
  `use-catalog-control-state.ts` owns selection and local draft state, and
  values, inspector, mutation-dialog, and current-value rendering live in
  focused files. Catalog classification, draft validation, and mutation
  transitions live under `work-model/catalog/`; command submission lives under
  `local-runtime/commands/`.
- `presentation/workflows/work-design/artifacts/context-brief/` is the correct
  artifact boundary. Finalized brief projection, fingerprinting, snapshot
  attachment modeling, board snapshot generation, finalized brief rendering,
  evidence wrapping, record-reference details, saved-session state, handoff
  rows, and finalization requirements live in focused files.
  `use-work-design-context-artifacts.ts` remains the composition hook.
- `presentation/workflows/work-design/session-controller/use-work-design-session-controller.ts`
  is an accepted composition root. It may be long when the length is
  explicit prop wiring between already-focused hooks, panels, product hosts,
  lifecycle, apply, and dialog owners. Split only a proven hidden owner, not
  the composition root itself.
- `presentation/workflows/work-design/session/work-design-session-step-router.tsx`
  and `presentation/workflows/work-design/session/work-design-session-dialogs.tsx`
  are accepted composition roots. They may unpack focused hook outputs and
  route props into owned step/dialog components, but must not absorb independent
  panel rendering, projection families, or command authority.
- `local-runtime/commands/` owns prototype-local command receipt factories.
  Workflow presentation may keep modal/session state, but must not fabricate
  command-result receipts inline when a runtime helper exists.
- `local-runtime/persistence/` owns prototype-local persistence. Persistence
  shape changes are out of scope for Delivery structure normalization and need
  a separate recorded design decision before implementation.
- `local-runtime/projections/` owns prototype-local cross-surface projection
  overlays. Presentation may subscribe to projection snapshots, but must not
  keep local Delivery package/intake/workflow projection truth in component
  state.
- `work-model/work-design/work-design-types.ts` keeps persisted snapshot/session
  and artifact-oriented Context Board DTO aliases only.
  Transient app interaction aliases used by Work Design session code are
  imported from the Context Board product-adapter facade instead.
- Refinement's metadata draft step keeps many subcomponents locally. That is
  acceptable only while those components are truly step-local. Any product-app
  viewer/selector host behavior must be classified as a host edge.
- Execution Board's Control Board integration is a product-app boundary case.
  It must not be used as precedent for casual product-app imports elsewhere.

